# stdout — redirection examples

## Redirect stderr to stdout

```sh
some-command 2>&1
```

## Send both stdout and stderr to the same file

```sh
cp -v * &> copy.log
```

## Discard all output

```sh
noisy-command &> /dev/null
```

## Send only stderr to a file

```sh
some-command 2> errors.txt
```

## Append instead of overwrite

```sh
some-command >> run.log 2>&1
```

## Log everything and still watch it live

```sh
long-job 2>&1 | tee job.log
```

## Reference

| Syntax      | Meaning                              |
| ----------- | ------------------------------------ |
| `>`         | redirect stdout (overwrite)          |
| `>>`        | redirect stdout (append)             |
| `2>`        | redirect stderr                      |
| `2>&1`      | send stderr wherever stdout goes     |
| `&>`        | send both stdout and stderr          |
| `/dev/null` | the bit bucket — discard output      |
